---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { exampleCount, pyodide, rangeShort, site, snippets, stdlibRows, idiomRows, url } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="About" description="What “still pass” means, how CI checks it, and where the material comes from.">
  <article class="about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <span class="label">About</span>
      <h1>What “still pass” means.</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Snippet sites go stale quietly. A result written in a comment looks the same whether or not it is still true. Here, every example is a
        doctest: the <code class="i">&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> line is run, and its output has to match what the page shows, character for character.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is checked</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Each of the {snippets.length} functions lives in a real module, <code class="i">src/pysnippets/&lt;package&gt;/&lt;module&gt;.py</code>. Its
          docstring holds the examples you see on the page, {exampleCount} in all.
        </li>
        <li>
          CI runs <code class="i">pytest --doctest-modules</code> on Python {site.pythons.join(', ')}. A page whose examples fail on any of them doesn't
          ship.
        </li>
        <li>
          The code is type-checked with <code class="i">mypy --strict</code> and linted with <code class="i">ruff</code>, and the proofs on the
          <a href={url('stdlib/')}>stdlib cheat sheet</a> ({stdlibRows.length} rows) and the <a href={url('idioms/')}>JS idioms</a> page
          ({idiomRows.length} rows) run as doctests too.
        </li>
        <li>
          The code and examples on each page are rebuilt from the module by <code class="i">snip sync</code>, and CI fails if they differ, so
          the page and the tested file can't drift apart.
        </li>
        <li>
          The ✓ next to an example means it passed on Python {rangeShort()}. The <b>Run in your browser</b> button runs the same doctests on Python
          {pyodide.python} through <a href="https://pyodide.org/">Pyodide</a>, and turns them red if you break something.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where the material comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Most snippets are adapted from <a href={site.upstreamRepo}>30-seconds-of-python</a> by the 30 seconds of code contributors, used under
        <a href={site.ccby}>CC BY 4.0</a>. Each adapted page names its originals and says what changed. Many were rewritten: typed, made to handle
        empty input, or replaced by a standard library call. <a href={site.attribution}>ATTRIBUTION.md</a> accounts for all {site.upstreamTotal}
        originals.
      </p>
      <p>
        py-snippets is an independent project by Ahmed Hamdy. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by 30 seconds of code, and uses none of its
        names, logos or images as branding.
      </p>

      <h2>License</h2>
      <p>
        The project is MIT licensed. Adapted material stays under CC BY 4.0. The source, the tests and the site are on
        <a href={site.repo}>GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
